{"content":{"activity_type":"event_msg/item_completed","completed_at_ms":1790216188545,"item":{"aggregated_output":"   210\t## Adapters\n   211\t\n   212\tSupported today (built-in, per-turn, triggered by the CLI's own hook or\n   213\tplugin mechanism):\n   214\t\n   215\tEvery captured event records the agent that served it — `producer.model`,\n   216\t`producer.provider`, `producer.source_version` — but only where the source\n   217\tstates it; see the Roadmap entry below for what each adapter does and does\n   218\tnot know.\n   219\t\n   220\t| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n   221\t|---|---|---|---|\n   222\t| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. Every line type is now recorded: `system` lines that printed text become turns spoken by the system, `attachment` becomes `context_injection`, `mode`/`permission-mode`/`worktree-state`/`pr-link` and friends become `session_state`, `queue-operation` and the rest become `activity`, and `file-history-*` becomes `file_snapshot` with backup digests resolved at capture time. Sidechain (subagent) turns are captured as sub-conversations instead of dropped — including the `<session>/subagents/agent-*.jsonl` sibling files newer Claude Code writes, which the hook payload never names. The hook also sweeps other transcripts in the same project that cledger already has a cursor for and whose file grew past it, which is how a session's final lines get captured at all: the last hook reads to EOF, and Claude Code writes the closing bookkeeping (including the session's most complete file-history snapshot) afterwards. A transcript with no cursor is left alone — it was never cledger's to capture, and adopting it would backfill old conversations against today's HEAD. |\n   223\t| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Provider-encrypted `reasoning` items are preserved opaquely as `reasoning`-kind events (hidden from `log`/`show` by default, see Roadmap); inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured as two events at the same `seq`: the visible turn, and a sealed `reasoning`-kind sibling carrying the embedded `encrypted_content` blocks, so an inter-agent session can still be replayed back through the provider that encrypted it. Sub-agent threads get their own rollout file, indistinguishable by name, and are captured as sub-conversations of the thread that spawned them (a session is keyed on `session_meta.payload.id`, since a child's `session_id` field names its *parent*); cledger does not yet walk `sub_agent_activity`'s `agent_thread_id` to find those files, so a child is captured only if something hands cledger its rollout. `turn_context` and `session_meta` are now recorded as `session_state` as well as read for `producer` metadata, so the sandbox policy, approval policy and reasoning effort a turn ran under are part of the record. Of the `event_msg` UI stream, only `agent_message`/`user_message` are dropped — they genuinely duplicate a `response_item`; token counts, task lifecycle, sub-agent activity, patch application and aborts have no twin and are recorded as `activity`. |\n   224\t| opencode | `session.idle` plugin event | SQLite at `~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db` | The only adapter without an append-only transcript file. Rather than read opencode's database — whose schema is mid-migration, and which also stores API tokens — capture shells out to `opencode export --pure <id>` and converts its JSON. One event per *part*, not per message: opencode's store is mutable, and a part is the finest unit that stops changing, so a message that gains parts after an early idle appends rather than duplicating. `conversation.seq` and the capture cursor both come from the part id (a 48-bit creation timestamp opencode encodes in it), so deleting a part out of the middle renumbers nothing — a session whose ids opencode did not mint falls back to positional numbering. Unlike Codex, `reasoning` parts are plaintext, so they become ordinary visible `thinking` blocks. Unsettled (`running`) tool calls are skipped and hold the cursor back until they finish. Subagent sessions are captured as their own conversation: opencode records a child's session id in the parent's `task` tool part, which is the only way to find them at all (`opencode session list` returns top-level sessions only), so capture walks them depth-first. Requires `opencode` on `PATH`; `cledger capture opencode --all` backfills every session opencode scopes to the current project, and `--transcript` reads a saved export. |\n   225\t| Gemini CLI | `AfterAgent`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.gemini/tmp/<project>/chats/session-*.jsonl` | The session file is append-only but is *not* a transcript: it is a write-ahead log of mutations to one conversation document — `$rewindTo` removals, `$set` metadata patches, whole-document `$set.messages` snapshots, and message upserts keyed by a durable id. Capture replays it with Gemini's own record precedence but deliberately does **not** honour the removals: verified on a live session, Gemini rewrote the document after a failed API call and silently dropped the prompt the user had just typed, so a faithful replay records no trace of it having been asked. The ledger keeps the union of every message the file ever held, each at its last-written value, and records each withdrawal as an `activity` naming the ids it took out — `rewind` for an explicit `$rewindTo`, `snapshot_drop` for a `$set.messages` snapshot that quietly omits what the document used to hold — so a withdrawn turn is legible as withdrawn rather than merely present. Both paths matter: the snapshot is the one the live incident above actually took. Verified against live Gemini CLI 0.54.0 sessions — including one where Gemini **deleted its own session file** after a `/rewind`, leaving the ledger's copy of that conversation the only surviving record of it. `$set` patches become `session_state` (with the message list left out of `raw`, since Gemini rewrites the *whole* conversation into every snapshot and each message is already captured as its own event), `info`/`error`/`warning` notices become turns spoken by the system (Gemini discards them; \"quota exceeded\" is often the only explanation for why a turn looks abandoned). Because nothing is ever removed, `seq` is assigned at first sighting and never reused, so a rewind cannot renumber later turns — the positional churn the opencode adapter accepts does not arise here. A model turn whose tool calls have not settled is withheld and holds the cursor back. Sub-sessions filed under `<chats>/<parent session id>/` are captured as sub-conversations. Gemini's harness-authored `<session_context>`/`<hook_context>` preambles are recorded but attributed to `system`, not to you. |\n   226\t| Qwen Code | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.qwen/projects/<escaped-cwd>/chats/<session-id>.jsonl` | A fork of Gemini CLI that did *not* inherit its conversation store: plain append-only JSONL with a claude-code-shaped envelope, so capture is per line with a line-index `seq`, a torn-tail-safe cursor, and the same catch-up sweep claude-code runs. Content is a Google GenAI `Part` list (shared with the Gemini CLI adapter): a `thought` part becomes a visible `thinking` block, `functionCall`/`functionResponse` become `tool_use`/`tool_result`. Everything that is not a turn arrives as a `system` line discriminated by `subtype`, and each maps to a kind: `attribution_snapshot` becomes `file_snapshot` (with per-file content hashes already in the record, so nothing needs resolving against a local cache), `custom_title`/`session_source`/`parent_session`/`goal_state` become `session_state`, `at_command` and the agent-launch prompts become `context_injection`, and everything else — including a subtype a future Qwen adds — becomes `activity` rather than being dropped or guessed at. A `parent_session` record makes a subagent chat a sub-conversation of the session that spawned it — though on Qwen Code 0.21.5 that is **not** how delegation was observed to work: a subagent runs in-process behind an `agent` tool call, writes no chat file of its own and no `parent_session` record, so the ledger stores the delegation as an ordinary `tool_use`/`tool_result` pair in the parent. Nothing about it is lost — the prompt handed to the subagent, its status, terminate reason and a per-tool execution summary all ride in `toolCallResult.resultDisplay` and are preserved in `raw` — but the subagent's own turn-by-turn conversation is never written by Qwen at all, so no adapter can recover it. Assistant lines state the serving model; `provider` is left unset because Qwen records only an *auth mode* (`openai`, `qwen-oauth`), not the inference provider the schema asks for. |\n   227\t\n   228\tTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n   229\tstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n   230\tsystems):\n   231\t\n   232\t- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/sessions/**/agents/<agent>/wire.jsonl`, an append-only\n   233\t  wire log with a `protocol_version` header, one directory per agent (so subagents are\n   234\t  separated at the filesystem level); Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n   235\t- **Google Antigravity** — where Google now sends individual users whose Gemini CLI\n   236\t  `oauth-personal` sign-in is refused (see the Gemini CLI note below). Unexamined:\n   237\t  whether it keeps a local session store worth capturing is unknown, and this list\n   238\t  does not guess. If it does, it is a *sibling* of the Gemini CLI adapter rather than\n   239\t  a replacement — that CLI is still published, still writes the same format, and is\n   240\t  still supported here.\n   241\t- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n   242\t- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n   243\t- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n   244\t- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n   245\t- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n   246\t\n   247\t## Security & redaction\n   248\t\n   249\tVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n   250\t\n   251\t### What cledger does and does not protect\n   252\t\n   253\tThe one enforced guarantee: **an event that a scan rule or a human flagged does not leave this machine over the ledger ref.** The pre-push gate is the enforcement point — everything earlier (capture-time masking) is best-effort harm reduction, and everything the gate never sees is out of scope. Concretely out of scope:\n   254\t\n   255\t- **Your local disk.** The agent CLIs' own transcripts hold the same content in plaintext (`~/.claude/projects/**`, `~/.codex/sessions/**`, …) whether or not cledger exists. Ledger notes are no more and no less exposed locally than the transcripts they came from.\n   256\t- **Secrets an agent reads because you let it.** An agent that can `cat ~/.aws/credentials` will put the result in its transcript, and no pattern list catches every secret shape. Set file permissions so agents (which run as you) have nothing to find: keep real credentials in a password manager or OS keychain, not in world- or user-readable dotfiles inside repos you run agents in.\n   257\t- **Secrets you paste into a conversation.** Capture-tier rules catch well-known token formats; a pasted connection string or a bespoke internal token may sail through to the note. Treat an agent conversation like a group chat: if you would not paste it there, do not paste it here.\n   258\t- **Other exits.** `cledger export`, `log`, `show`, and the raw notes ref all print stored content freely on the machine that holds it — they are reads of local plaintext, not shares. The gate sits on the one path that publishes: pushing the ledger ref.\n   259\t\n   260\tIf a real secret does reach the remote, redaction cannot recall it (see `cledger redact` below for why) — **rotate the credential**. That is the honest failure mode of every system in this category.\n   261\t\n   262\t### Writing secret-shaped fixtures\n   263\t\n   264\tRepos that test or document secret handling (this one included) constantly mint fake credentials, and every fake that looks real enough becomes a scan finding a human has to wave through. Two conventions keep fixtures out of the review queue *at authoring time* instead:\n   265\t\n   266\t- **Put an uppercase marker in the fake value**: `password=FAKEhunter2aa`, `api_key: \"EXAMPLE-abcdef12345678\"`. Scan-tier heuristics skip spans containing `FAKE`, `EXAMPLE`, `PLACEHOLDER`, `DUMMY`, `NOTREAL`, or `TESTONLY` (uppercase only — `example.com` in a URL does not count, writing the marker must be an authoring choice). This is the convention to teach your coding agents; this repo's `CLAUDE.md` does.\n   267\t- **For format-valid tokens** (a syntactically real `ghp_…` that must exercise a capture rule), markers are ignored by design — store the token split into parts and reassemble at runtime, as `src/test/fixtures/secret-corpus.json` does with `secret_parts`. A whole format-valid token should never exist as literal bytes in a file, a conversation, or a commit.\n   268\t\n   269\t### Redaction layers\n   270\t\n   271\t**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n   272\t- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n   273\t- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n   274\t\n   275\t**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n   276\t- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n   277\t- Cost: manual configuration required.\n   278\t\n   279\t**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n   280\t- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n   281\t- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n   282\t\n   283\t**Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n   284\t- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n   285\t- Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n   286\t\n   287\t**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report prints **coordinates only** — event id, rule, JSON path + offset, fingerprint — and never a character of the flagged text or its surroundings, because the context around a match is itself what tripped the rule, so a printed excerpt re-seeds a finding on the report. The report is **grouped by fingerprint**: the same span recurring across an event's `content` and `raw` mirrors, or across every edit of one file, is one decision, and the report's size tracks decisions rather than match sites (this repo's own dogfood backlog was 153 sites that collapse to 11 spans). Readable content lives behind `cledger review` (interactive, one screen per span) and `cledger inspect <event-id>` (writes a `0600` file outside the repo); both refuse to run inside a coding-agent session. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n   288\t- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n   289\t- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n   290\t- Remediation paths: `cledger review` (walk every outstanding span, one keystroke each), `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n   291\t- Scan-tier heuristics skip a match whose span carries an uppercase fixture marker (`FAKE`, `EXAMPLE`, `PLACEHOLDER`, `DUMMY`, `NOTREAL`, `TESTONLY`) — see \"Writing secret-shaped fixtures\" below. Capture-tier rules never honor the marker: they match real token formats, and a real leaked token could contain those bytes by chance.\n   292\t\n   293\t**Allowlist tiers**: a false positive is usually a property of the *text* (a fixture, a doc example), not of the repo it was captured in — the same span gets re-flagged in the next repo that quotes the same doc. `cledger allow <fp>` records it for this repo (`.git/conversation-ledger/allowlist.json`); `cledger allow <fp> --global` records it for every repo on this machine (`~/.config/cledger/allowlist.json`); and a repo can commit shared entries in `.cledger.json` as `{\"scan\": {\"allowFingerprints\": [\"...\"]}}` so clones and teammates inherit them. All three union at scan time. Fingerprints are truncated `sha256` of a span a human judged *not* to be a secret, so committing them discloses nothing.\n   294\t\n   295\t### Commands\n   296\t\n   297\t`cledger review [--paranoid] [--context N]`: The intended remediation path. Steps through every outstanding finding interactively — one screen per distinct span, the match highlighted inside real surrounding context, `a` to allow in this repo, `g` to allow globally, `r` to redact it everywhere it appears (the span is escaped into a literal pattern for you — you never retype a secret), `s` to skip. HUMANS ONLY: refuses inside a coding-agent session and without a TTY on both ends, for the same reason inspect does — flagged content that lands in a transcript re-seeds the finding.\n   298\t\n   299\t`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites a stored event to a placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and severs the local notes-ref commit chain so the prior note is unreachable from the ref.\n   300\t\n   301\t**Pre-push only, and enforced.** The command refuses once the target event is on `origin`, because rewriting shared content does not remove it: a notes ref carries its own commit chain, so every prior note body is in any clone's history (`git log -p refs/notes/conversation-ledger`), and `cat_sort_uniq` unions *lines*, so the original returns on the next merge — a scoped push would in fact re-upload it alongside the rewrite. It also refuses when `origin` cannot be reached, rather than assuming nothing was shared. Gating is per *event*, so pushing regularly does not disable redaction for anything captured afterwards. For a secret that has already left the machine, rotate the credential; purging shared ledger history is separate destructive tooling that does not exist yet.\n   302\t\n   303\tSevering the chain is not an object purge: the old blobs stay in the local reflog and object store until git's own expiry + `gc`. That residue is local-only (git transfers neither reflogs nor unreachable objects), and the command says so rather than claiming the value is gone. To drop them immediately: `git reflog expire --expire=now --all && git gc --prune=now` (repo-wide).\n   304\t\n   305\t`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n   306\t\n   307\t### Maximum safety recipe\n   308\t\n   309\tKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n   310\t\n   311\t## Roadmap\n   312\t\n   313\t- **Auto-triage of findings whose span is committed repo content — considered\n   314\t  and deferred, with data** (2026-08-12). The idea: if a flagged span exists\n   315\t  verbatim in a blob already on the remote, the ledger leaks nothing the repo\n   316\t  has not published, so the finding could downgrade itself. Tested against\n   317\t  the only backlog available — this repo's 11 outstanding fingerprints plus\n   318\t  turnbridge's 4 — it would have cleared **zero of 15**: the split-parts\n   319\t  corpus convention means assembled fixture bytes never reach a committed\n   320\t  file, and the other spans lived in shell commands and intermediate edit\n   321\t  states that were rewritten before committing. The mechanism is sound but\n   322\t  its hit rate on real backlogs is unproven, and it adds a scan-time\n   323\t  dependency on remote-reachability computation. Revisit if a backlog ever\n   324\t  shows committed-content spans; `cledger review` clearing 15 spans in a\n   325\t  minute weakens the case for more machinery.\n   326\t- **`cledger review` shows a context window, not the conversation** — the\n   327\t  0.21.0 reviewer renders the flagged string with ±600 chars (scrollable,\n   328\t  the whole string is available). A richer version would page through the\n   329\t  *conversation* around the event — prior and following turns, like reading\n   330\t  the transcript — which needs event-sequence pagination keyed on\n   331\t  `conversation.seq` and a real layout pass. Deferred until the per-span\n   332\t  flow proves insufficient; most verdicts so far need only the surrounding\n   333\t  string.\n   334\t- **The `>/dev/null 2>&1` on the Gemini and Qwen hooks was pointless, and cost\n   335\t  visibility** *(shipped, 0.22.0)* — it was added after `qwen -p \"...\"` failed\n   336\t  to capture, on the theory that the redirect \"routes the command through a\n   337\t  shell so the work outlives the teardown\". That theory is wrong: both CLIs\n   338\t  *always* invoke a hook as `bash -c \"<command>\"`, and `bash -c` `exec`s a\n   339\t  single simple command rather than forking, so nothing was ever detached. The\n   340\t  actual cause of hooks dying was the timeout unit below. All the redirect ever\n   341\t  did was hide the capture's own output, so a format-drift warning had nowhere\n   342\t  to print and `cledger capture <source> --all` was the only way to watch one.\n   343\t  New installs no longer write it, and `cledger install` **strips it from an\n   344\t  existing hook in place** — the same contract the timeout repair established:\n   345\t  a setting cledger itself got wrong should be fixable by re-running install,\n   346\t  not by hand-editing JSON. Only cledger's own hook is touched, and only when\n   347\t  the command ends in that exact string, so any other redirect a user wrote\n   348\t  survives. *Still unverified:* the original headless `qwen -p` case has not\n   349\t  been re-run, because that needs a live Qwen session; the reasoning above\n   350\t  says it was never the redirect that saved it, but nobody has watched it\n   351\t  since.\n   352\t- **`cledger log` hid human-driven records by default** *(shipped, 0.22.0)* —\n   353\t  the session-machinery kinds (`session_state`, `activity`,\n   354\t  `context_injection`, `file_snapshot`) were hidden unless you passed\n   355\t  `--with-state`. That default earns its keep against telemetry: one real Qwen\n   356\t  session emitted 57 `ui_telemetry` records against 45 events of actual\n   357\t  content, and an unfiltered default would bury the conversation. But it did\n   358\t  not distinguish *whose* action a record was, so a slash command the human\n   359\t  typed was hidden by the same rule as a token-count ping.\n   360\t  The display default is now keyed on the **actor**: a machinery event whose\n   361\t  actor is `human` shows, the harness's and the agent's bookkeeping stays\n   362\t  behind `--with-state`, and the flag's meaning is unchanged (show everything).\n   363\t  Capture is untouched and `cledger export` is still lossless — this only ever\n   364\t  changed what is displayed.\n   365\t  *The premise needed repair before the fix meant anything.* \"`activity`\n   366\t  already carries a real actor\" was half true: `activityDraft` was the only one\n   367\t  of the four builders that even took an actor, and exactly one call site in\n   368\t  the whole codebase passed `human` (Qwen's `slash_command`). Shipping only the\n   369\t  display change would have surfaced that one record and nothing else. Two\n   370\t  attributions were corrected alongside it, both where the source *states* who\n   371\t  acted: Gemini's `$rewindTo` is the person saying \"take that back\" (its\n   372\t  sibling `snapshot_drop` — Gemini quietly rewriting its own document — stays\n   373\t  `system`, and that difference is the entire reason the ledger records them as\n   374\t  two things), and Qwen's `at_command` is a person naming a file, which was\n   375\t  landing in `context_injection` with the identical `system` actor as\n   376\t  `agent_bootstrap`, the harness's own preamble that nobody typed.\n   377\t  `contextInjectionDraft` grew an actor parameter for that, matching\n   378\t  `activityDraft`.\n   379\t  *Deliberately not corrected: claude-code's `queue-operation`.* It looked like\n   380\t  the same fix — a queued message is something a human typed mid-turn — but the\n   381\t  source does not say so. Measured across 40 local transcripts: `enqueue`\n   382\t  splits 126 harness-generated to 43 human-typed, and `remove` 70 to 16. The\n   383\t  only available discriminator is a content-shape heuristic (harness ones\n   384\t  arrive wrapped in a `task-notification` envelope), which is a convention that\n   385\t  can change, and getting it wrong files machine-authored text under a named\n   386\t  person's git identity — the failure this whole entry is about, in the\n   387\t  direction that actually does damage. Attribution follows what the source\n   388\t  states; where it must be guessed from content, it stays `system`. Revisit if\n   389\t  claude-code ever labels the queue entry's origin.\n   390\t  *Forward-only, like every attribution change here.* `actor.type` and\n   391\t  `actor.id` are part of event identity, so a re-capture of an\n   392\t  already-captured session mints new ids for the re-attributed records rather\n   393\t  than deduping. That is id churn in one adapter's machinery records, not lost\n   394\t  content, and there is no backfill — rewriting stored events would put two\n   395\t  copies of each through `cat_sort_uniq` on the next merge with any peer.\n   396\t- **Gemini CLI's version is not in its session file** — claude-code and\n   397\t  qwen-code stamp the CLI version onto every line, so `producer.source_version`\n   398\t  comes for free. Gemini stamps none, and neither the hook payload nor the\n   399\t  session header carries one, so gemini-cli events have no `source_version`\n   400\t  unless a caller passes it. Reading it off the installed binary would be\n   401\t  wrong for a backfill of an old session, and guessing is worse than omitting.\n   402\t  Fixing it properly means asking Gemini to record it, or accepting a\n   403\t  capture-time \"the binary on PATH said 0.53.1\" annotation that is explicitly\n   404\t  about the capture rather than the session.\n   405\t- **`timeout` means different units in Claude Code than in the CLIs that copied\n   406\t  its hook format** — Claude Code reads a command hook's `timeout` as seconds;\n   407\t  Gemini CLI and Qwen Code pass the same field straight to `setTimeout`, so\n   408\t  theirs is milliseconds (`Hook timed out after ${timeout}ms`,\n   409\t  `DEFAULT_HOOK_TIMEOUT = 6e4`). `cledger install` wrote Claude's `120` into all\n   410\t  three, so on Gemini and Qwen every hook was SIGTERMed after 120 *milliseconds*\n   411\t  — less than a cold capture takes. The symptom was a CLI reporting\n   412\t  `Hook(s) [...] failed` on nearly every turn while its events sometimes still\n   413\t  landed, because whether the append finished before the signal was a race.\n   414\t  Fixed by writing each CLI's own unit, and `install` now repairs an existing\n   415\t  hook's timeout in place rather than seeing \"already installed\" and doing\n   416\t  nothing. If another CLI adopts this config shape, check its unit before\n   417\t  assuming.\n   418\t- **Signing in to Gemini CLI now needs a key** — Google refuses\n   419\t  `oauth-personal` sign-in for individuals on this client (*\"no longer\n   420\t  supported for Gemini Code Assist for individuals… migrate to the Antigravity\n   421\t  suite\"*), and that refusal is server-side, not a client change. The shipped\n   422\t  CLI still supports `gemini-api-key`, `vertex-ai` and `cloud-shell`, and\n   423\t  honours `GOOGLE_GEMINI_BASE_URL`. Note it has no OpenAI-compatible mode,\n   424\t  unlike Qwen Code (`OPENAI_BASE_URL`), so a local endpoint has to speak the\n   425\t  Gemini API. The transcript format is stable across the change — Gemini CLI\n   426\t  0.54.0's record predicates are byte-identical to 0.53.1's.\n   427\t- **Most Qwen `system` subtypes are classified by name, not by observed shape**\n   428\t  — live sessions have now exercised `ui_telemetry`, `attribution_snapshot`,\n   429\t  `file_history_snapshot`, `at_command` and `slash_command`. The rest of the\n   430\t  mapping (`custom_title` and `goal_state` → `session_state`,\n   430\t  mapping (`custom_title` and `goal_state` → `session_state`,\n   431\t  `agent_bootstrap`/`agent_launch_prompt` → `context_injection`, and the\n   432\t  subtypes that fall through to `activity`) is still read off Qwen's own\n   433\t  `KNOWN_RECORD_SUBTYPES` list rather than off a transcript that produced them.\n   434\t  Notably `/chat save <title>` did *not* emit `custom_title` — it emitted a\n   435\t  pair of `slash_command` records — so the `custom_title` path remains\n   436\t  unobserved and may belong to some other gesture. Fields pass through verbatim\n   437\t  either way, so the risk is a record filed under a slightly wrong kind, not\n   438\t  lost or mangled content, but a subtype that turns out to be state while\n   439\t  sitting in `activity` would be invisible to a consumer filtering on\n   440\t  `session_state`.\n   441\t- **`cledger export | head` died with an unhandled EPIPE** *(shipped, 0.22.0)*\n   442\t  — `printJsonl` wrote to `process.stdout` with no `error` handler, so when the\n   443\t  reader went away mid-write Node threw and printed a stack trace instead of\n   444\t  exiting quietly. Not exotic: piping any ledger larger than the 64KB pipe\n   445\t  buffer into `head`, or into a command that fails at startup, reproduced it —\n   446\t  `cledger export | head -1` against a 23MB ledger threw every time. Small\n   447\t  ledgers hid it, because the whole payload fits the buffer before the reader\n   448\t  exits and no write ever fails.\n   449\t  Fixed with an `EPIPE` handler that exits 0, the convention every\n   450\t  `head`-friendly CLI follows; any other stdout error still reports and exits\n   451\t  1. The handler is installed for the whole CLI rather than around the three\n   452\t  JSON printers, since `log`'s human format and `conversations` write to the\n   453\t  same stdout and broke the same way.\n   454\t  *The second half was less obvious.* `write()` reports EPIPE\n   455\t  **asynchronously**, so a synchronous print loop runs to completion after the\n   456\t  reader is already gone — the handler fires, but only once the work is\n   457\t  finished and the whole ledger has been buffered in memory. The bulk printers\n   458\t  therefore honour backpressure (`await once(stdout, \"drain\")` when `write()`\n   459\t  returns false), which is what makes the exit prompt rather than merely quiet.\n   460\t  Regression-tested by spawning the real binary and closing the pipe after the\n   461\t  first chunk: an in-process call has no pipe to break, and a fixture under\n   462\t  64KB passes without the fix.\n   463\t- **opencode capture was positional over a mutable store** *(shipped, 0.25.0)*\n   464\t  — the other adapters read append-only transcript files, so `conversation.seq`\n   465\t  (the source line index) is stable forever and event ids never churn. opencode\n   466\t  keeps sessions in SQLite, where `session revert`, `message.removed` and\n   467\t  `part.removed` delete parts out of the middle. Deleting a part shifted the\n   468\t  positions of everything after it, and since `seq` is part of event identity,\n   469\t  the shifted parts re-captured under new ids — duplicates in the ledger rather\n   470\t  than lost content, but permanent, because the ledger is append-only.\n   471\t\n   472\t  *The open question was whether opencode's part ids are orderable. They are.*\n   473\t  opencode mints `prt_` + 12 hex + 14 random base62, where the hex is a 48-bit\n   474\t  `Date.now() * 4096 + per-millisecond counter` — its own `Identifier` scheme,\n   475\t  ULID-shaped without being a ULID. Verified against a live 1.18.10 store: 859\n   476\t  parts, **zero ordering violations within any session**. So `seq` now comes\n   477\t  from the part's own id, which makes it a property of the part rather than of\n   478\t  its neighbours: a deletion renumbers nothing and a rescan dedups.\n   479\t\n   480\t  *Chosen per session, never per part.* Ids not minted by opencode exist in the\n   481\t  wild (test harnesses and other tooling write their own), and a session mixing\n   482\t  a 48-bit timestamp with a small integer would sort into nonsense. Unless\n   483\t  every part in a session parses — and the values are distinct — the whole\n   484\t  session keeps the positional numbering and behaves exactly as it did before.\n   485\t\n   486\t  *The cursor moved to the same key.* It stored a part **count**, which only\n   487\t  notices parts going away when the session gets shorter; a deletion offset by\n   488\t  a new part left the count looking healthy while pointing at the wrong place.\n   489\t  It is now a high-water mark over `seq`, which has nothing to be offset by.\n   490\t  The \"an unsettled tool call holds the cursor back\" rule is unchanged in\n   491\t  effect — it just holds below the held part's `seq` instead of its index.\n   492\t\n   493\t  *Cost, accepted deliberately:* a 48-bit `seq` is not a counter, and printing\n   494\t  it raw turned every opencode row into `opencode:9f2a#67580218395`. `cledger\n   495\t  log`'s human format now prints a **position within the conversation**,\n   496\t  ranked over the events the read returned so that hiding the harness's\n   497\t  bookkeeping does not renumber the turns around it. `--json` and `export` are\n   498\t  untouched and still carry the stored `seq`: a machine reading the ledger\n   499\t  wants the real ordering key, not a per-view position. Ties share a rank,\n   500\t  since a reasoning blob and the turn it belongs to are deliberately written at\n   501\t  the same `seq`.\n   502\t\n   503\t  *Migration:* forward-only, like every identity change here. Already-captured\n   504\t  opencode events keep their positional seqs; only newly captured parts use the\n   505\t  derived ones, and the two sort correctly together (small numbers first, which\n   506\t  is also their real order). Re-capturing an old session from scratch would\n   507\t  file second copies — the same one-round cost every identity change in this\n   508\t  project carries, and bounded here by how few opencode events exist relative\n   509\t  to a whole ledger.\n   510\t\n   511\t  Related: opencode's `session delete` removes a conversation from opencode\n   512\t  entirely while the ledger keeps it, which is a feature, not a bug.\n   513\t- **The opencode hook's session-id fallback is untested** — the plugin reads\n   514\t  `event.properties.sessionID`, confirmed against a live `session.idle` from\n   515\t  opencode 1.18.5, so the normal path is pinned to the real field. The hook\n   516\t  still treats the id as optional and falls back to capturing the most\n   517\t  recently updated session for the directory, so a future opencode that\n   518\t  renames the field degrades instead of silently stopping. That fallback has\n   519\t  never run in anger, and it would pick the wrong session if two sessions in\n   520\t  one project went idle in the same instant. Worth a test that exercises it\n   521\t  directly, and a warning when it triggers, so a silent rename does not look\n   522\t  like normal operation.\n   523\t- **`opencode export` truncates a piped stdout at 64KB** — an upstream Bun\n   524\t  flush bug: the process exits without draining stdout, so any session over\n   525\t  ~64KB comes back cut off, and the truncated JSON can still parse. cledger\n   526\t  works around it by routing the child's stdout to a temp file rather than a\n   527\t  pipe (see `runOpencode`). Worth reporting upstream; the workaround should\n   528\t  stay regardless, since it costs nothing and protects older opencode\n   529\t  versions.\n   530\t- **Config sections replace rather than merge, and it fails open** — *fixed in\n   531\t  0.21.0*: `loadConfig` now merges each top-level section key-by-key, so a\n   532\t  repo `.cledger.json` that sets only `redact.patterns` no longer silently\n   533\t  reverts a globally-enabled `redact.knownSecrets` to its default. The one\n   534\t  deliberate exception: `scan.allowFingerprints` arrays are unioned rather\n   535\t  than repo-wins, because the allowlist is accumulative — a fingerprint either\n   536\t  config trusts is trusted, and replacement would make a repo-local entry\n   537\t  silently drop every personal one. Kept here rather than deleted because the\n   538\t  bug is a family member (see below) and its shape — a config surface where\n   539\t  *mentioning* a section disabled a sibling protection — is worth remembering\n   540\t  when adding config keys.\n   541\t\n   542\t  **Self-referential failures are now this project's characteristic bug\n   543\t  family**, and are worth a standing check rather than four independent\n   544\t  fixes. cledger records the work of building cledger, so its own state,\n   545\t  output, and documentation feed back into its behavior. Known members: the\n   546\t  capture/scan redaction loop (a finding report re-seeding the finding it\n   547\t  describes — closed in 0.6.1/0.7.0); the pre-0.13.0 worktree state split\n   548\t  (0.13.0); this config section-replace; and the allowlist entry below, whose\n   549\t  *write-up* of a false positive reproduces the false positive in every repo\n   550\t  whose conversations quote it. Three of the four fail silently, and two fail\n   551\t  open. A useful tripwire when adding anything that reads cledger's own\n   552\t  state or prints cledger's own findings: ask what happens when that output\n   553\t  is itself captured.\n   554\t- **Don't print findings by default; print a pointer to them** — the cleanest\n   555\t  structural break in the self-referential loop above. Today `cledger scan`\n   556\t  and the sync gate print every finding inline, which is exactly the text that\n   557\t  gets captured into the next conversation and re-seeds the finding. Masking\n   558\t  the matched span (0.6.1) removed the secret characters but not the\n   559\t  *surrounding* credential-shaped context, which is what\n   560\t  `keyword-assignment` trips on — so the loop is narrowed, not closed.\n   561\t  Proposed: report only the count and how to inspect, with the audience split\n   562\t  made explicit in the text — a human is told which command reveals the\n   563\t  findings; an agent is told not to run it, because reading the findings into\n   564\t  its context is what reproduces them, and to surface the count to its human\n   565\t  instead. Naming the failure mode in the output is the point: an agent that\n   566\t  understands *why* will comply, where a bare \"don't look\" invites a\n   567\t  workaround. Pairs naturally with the inspection command in the design doc's\n   568\t  open questions, which already has to write unmasked output to a file rather\n   569\t  than stdout for the same reason. *Tension to resolve first:* CI reads those\n   570\t  printed findings, and `cledger scan`'s nonzero exit plus inline report is\n   571\t  the documented CI integration — so the default likely has to be\n   572\t  terminal-aware, or CI has to opt in explicitly (`--report`), rather than\n   573\t  simply going quiet for everyone.\n   574\t- **Branch-scoped push** *(shipped, 0.14.0)* — reachability filtered *reads*\n   575\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, `conversations`), but the notes ref is a single ref\n   576\t  and a ref push sends all of it, so conversations from a branch that was\n   577\t  abandoned and never merged still reached `origin` and landed on every\n   578\t  collaborator's disk. Two scopes wearing one name, with push the odd one out:\n   579\t  storage was already per-commit and reads already per-branch.\n   580\t\n   581\t  Push now carries only the anchors a read at that rev resolves to. Storage is\n   582\t  unchanged (commit-anchored, one local ref, one shared remote ref) — only\n   583\t  push learns the granularity storage already had. `cledger sync` scopes to\n   584\t  the current branch by default, `--rev` scopes elsewhere, `--all` restores\n   585\t  the whole-ledger push. **No mapping is stored anywhere:** reachability is\n   586\t  recomputed at push time from the DAG, the same reason reads never stored it.\n   587\t\n   588\t  *Squash merges are the case that decides the implementation.* The scope\n   589\t  reuses `resolveAnchors` — the same function reads use — rather than a plain\n   590\t  `git rev-list`. A squash discards the source branch's commits, so its\n   591\t  conversations sit on anchors unreachable from the target, and only the\n   592\t  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the *surviving* commit) tie them\n   593\t  back. A rev-list-only scope would push those mapping events while leaving\n   594\t  behind the events they point at — dropping exactly what re-anchoring exists\n   595\t  to save. Covered by a test that fails against a rev-list-only scope.\n   596\t\n   597\t  *The destructive shortcut, recorded because it is the natural first\n   598\t  implementation.* A notes ref is a snapshot of the whole database, not an\n   599\t  append log: its tip tree maps every annotated commit to its note, so\n   600\t  whatever tree you push becomes the remote's entire notes database. Filtering\n   601\t  your local ref down to one branch and pushing that still fast-forwards — and\n   602\t  silently deletes every other branch's conversations from the remote's\n   603\t  current view (they survive in the ref's history but vanish from `git notes\n   604\t  show` and from any fresh clone). The scoped push therefore seeds a temp ref\n   605\t  from the remote's tip and unions its scope *into* that, so the push strictly\n   606\t  adds. Also covered by a test that fails against the filtered-tree version.\n   607\t\n   608\t  *Side benefit:* the pre-push scan gate now inspects only what the push would\n   609\t  carry, so a finding in a branch that is not being pushed no longer blocks\n   610\t  shipping unrelated work.\n   611\t\n   612\t  *Scope follows the refs actually being pushed* (0.15.0). The hook is fed\n   613\t  git's pre-push stdin — one line per ref — so `git push origin feat` from\n   614\t  another branch shares feat's conversations. 0.14.0 shipped with `</dev/null`\n   615\t  and scoped by `HEAD`, which shared nothing in that case. Ref deletions are\n   616\t  skipped (no tip to scope to), multiple refs union, and anything unusable\n   617\t  falls back to `HEAD` rather than to the whole ledger. Because this changes\n   618\t  the installed hook, `installHook` now replaces a stale cledger block in\n   619\t  place instead of leaving any file containing the marker untouched — only the\n   620\t  marked region is rewritten, so surrounding user content survives.\n   621\t\n   622\t  *Not addressed here:* the read default — `cledger export` still defaulted to\n   623\t  no reachability filter, and the fix belonged in `readEvents` rather than in\n   624\t  one CLI command, since library consumers (intent-recall) never go through\n   625\t  the CLI and would otherwise inherit the unscoped view. Closed in 0.23.0; see\n   626\t  the entry below.\n   627\t  Per-branch remote refs (`…-branches/<branch>` plus a wildcard fetch refspec)\n   628\t  were considered and are *not* needed; they would only add fetch-side\n   629\t  selectivity, letting a collaborator decline a branch's conversations\n   630\t  entirely. Worth revisiting only if that is ever wanted.\n   631\t- **Reads defaulted to unscoped** *(shipped, 0.23.0)* — `readEvents` applied\n   632\t  reachability only when the caller asked, and `cledger export` never asked, so\n   633\t  an automated consumer reading the ledger directly got every branch's\n   634\t  conversations — including work that was abandoned and never merged — unless\n   635\t  it knew to pass a rev. With push scoped since 0.14.0, this was the remaining\n   636\t  half of the same mismatch.\n   637\t  `ReadOptions.reachableFrom` now defaults to `HEAD`, and the fix sits in\n   638\t  `readEvents` rather than in `export` precisely because the consumers that\n   639\t  matter — intent-recall and anything else using the library — never go through\n   640\t  the CLI at all. `cledger export` gains `--all` alongside `--rev`, matching\n   641\t  `log`.\n   642\t  *The opt-out is `null`, not \"unset\".* `undefined` means \"the caller did not\n   643\t  say\" and resolves to `HEAD`; only an explicit `reachableFrom: null` asks for\n   644\t  the whole local ledger. That distinction is the whole point — the callers\n   645\t  that genuinely want everything are few and deliberate, while the ones that\n   646\t  silently got everything by not asking were the bug. Four say so at their call\n   647\t  site, each for the same reason: they must not depend on what is checked out.\n   648\t  `cledger scan` and `cledger review` (a secret does not stop being one because\n   649\t  its branch is not checked out), `cledger inspect` (an event id is a global\n   650\t  handle), and `renormalize` (otherwise it strands preserved lines on every\n   651\t  branch but the current one). `cledger show <id>` is the fifth: you named one\n   652\t  conversation, so reachability is not the question you asked.\n   653\t  *Consequence worth knowing:* after a `cledger sync --fetch`, conversations\n   654\t  anchored to commits you have not fetched are invisible by default — correct,\n   655\t  since you cannot reach them, but it looks like nothing arrived. `--all`\n   656\t  answers \"did it arrive\"; the default answers \"does it belong to this\n   657\t  history\". Two of the transport tests had been quietly conflating the two.\n   658\t- **A closed reader can report `ENOTCONN`, not just `EPIPE`** *(shipped,\n   659\t  0.23.0)* — a follow-up to 0.22.0's stdout guard, which matched `EPIPE` alone.\n   660\t  On macOS Node does not always back stdio with a pipe, and a write to a\n   661\t  socket-backed stdout whose peer has closed reports `ENOTCONN` instead. So\n   662\t  `cledger log --json | head -1` still exited 1 with `write ENOTCONN` — the\n   663\t  stack trace was gone, the nonzero exit was not. Surfaced only under enough\n   664\t  concurrent load to change how far the teardown had progressed, which is why\n   665\t  the original test suite passed. `ECONNRESET` is matched too, for the\n   666\t  half-closed case. Which errno you get is an accident of what kind of file\n   667\t  descriptor Node handed the process, never of what the user did; the test now\n   668\t  asserts stderr is *empty* rather than free of one named errno, since an\n   669\t  assertion naming one would have passed while the command was still broken.\n   670\t- **Audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns** — this\n   671\t  repo's own dogfood allowlist (2026-07-22) picked up two `keyword-assignment`\n   672\t  fingerprints from this project's own development conversation: test code\n   673\t  like `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")`, where `\"github-token\"` is\n   674\t  a rule-id label, not a credential, but still trips \"a variable named\n   675\t  secret/token gets assigned something.\" Allowlisting is correct for now, but\n   676\t  it's a per-fingerprint, per-repo escape hatch — every downstream consumer of\n   677\t  cledger whose codebase talks *about* secrets (redaction tooling, security\n   678\t  tests, docs) will hit the same class of false positive independently, with\n   679\t  no shared fix. Periodically review what's accumulated in the allowlist: a\n   680\t  recurring pattern (e.g. \"the matched span is itself a rule id/label string,\n   681\t  not a real-looking token\") is a signal the `keyword-assignment` rule itself\n   682\t  should get smarter — not something every project should have to\n   683\t  allowlist for itself.\n   684\t  *0.21.0 delivered the shared-fix half*: the uppercase fixture marker keeps\n   685\t  deliberate fakes out of the queue entirely, `allow --global` and\n   686\t  `scan.allowFingerprints` in `.cledger.json` let one human decision travel\n   687\t  across repos and clones. The \"make the rule smarter\" half (a\n   688\t  discussion-vs-assignment discriminator) remains open below.\n   689\t  - **The false positive is self-perpetuating through documentation**\n   690\t    (observed 2026-07-27, from `intent-recall`): a design conversation that\n   691\t    quoted *this very roadmap entry* — the paragraph above, containing the\n   692\t    example `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")` — was itself flagged\n   693\t    with 6 `keyword-assignment` findings and held back from push. The\n   694\t    write-up of a false positive reproduces the false positive, in a\n   695\t    different repository, in a conversation transcript rather than in code.\n   696\t    Two things this exposes that the parent entry does not: (a) the allowlist\n   697\t    is per-repo, so a fingerprint retired here is re-encountered from scratch\n   698\t    by every downstream repo whose conversations read or discuss these docs —\n   699\t    and cledger's own documentation is now a reliable source of them; (b) the\n   700\t    flagged span was prose *about* a credential-shaped example and never a\n   700\t    flagged span was prose *about* a credential-shaped example and never a\n   701\t    credential, which is about as clean a signal as exists that the rule\n   702\t    wants a \"is this a discussion or an assignment?\" discriminator rather\n   703\t    than more allowlisting. Cheap partial fix worth weighing: treat a match\n   704\t    whose surrounding context is natural-language prose (a markdown\n   705\t    paragraph, a conversation turn) far more skeptically than one inside a\n   706\t    code block or a source file.\n   707\t- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n   708\t  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n   709\t  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n   710\t- **Redaction is pre-push only, and now enforced** *(shipped)* — `cledger\n   711\t  redact` used to detect the already-pushed case, warn, and proceed anyway,\n   712\t  reporting partial success. That was worse than useless: `pushScopedNotes`\n   713\t  seeds from the remote's tip and unions the local scope into it, so\n   714\t  redact-then-push *re-uploaded* the original alongside the rewrite, leaving\n   715\t  both copies on the remote permanently — and since a notes ref carries its\n   716\t  own commit chain, every clone could already recover the pre-redaction body\n   717\t  with a plain `git log -p refs/notes/conversation-ledger`. The command now\n   718\t  refuses outright, before touching anything, when the target event is on\n   719\t  `origin` — or when `origin` cannot be reached to rule it out (a security\n   720\t  decision made on a failed network call must fail closed; previously an\n   721\t  offline `ls-remote` was indistinguishable from a virgin remote). Gating is\n   722\t  per *event*, not per repo, so an earlier push does not disable redaction\n   723\t  for everything captured afterwards.\n   724\t  Output no longer claims `history squashed: yes`. Severing the notes chain\n   725\t  is real but is not an object purge — the old blobs remain in the local\n   726\t  reflog and object store until git's expiry + `gc` — so the command now\n   727\t  states exactly that, plus the fact that the residue is local-only (git\n   728\t  transfers neither reflogs nor unreachable objects) and the one-liner that\n   729\t  clears it. Running a repo-wide destructive `gc` on the user's behalf stays\n   730\t  out of scope.\n   731\t- **Reads must never prefer a pre-redaction copy** *(shipped)* — `dedupById`\n   732\t  collapses same-id copies by earliest `recorded_at`, then by a canonical-JSON\n   733\t  byte compare. A redacted event and its original share an id *by design*\n   734\t  (the rewrite pins it, so a rescan dedups instead of resurrecting the\n   735\t  secret) and, because the rewrite spreads the original, share `recorded_at`\n   736\t  too — so both keys tied and the byte compare decided, resolving on the\n   737\t  secret's first character against the `[` (0x5B) opening `[REDACTED:…]`.\n   738\t  Every secret starting with a digit or an uppercase letter sorted below it\n   739\t  and won, so reads printed the plaintext next to the `redaction` event\n   740\t  asserting its removal. Redaction depth is now consulted first: the copy\n   741\t  carrying more redaction records wins outright. Unambiguous, because\n   742\t  capture-time redaction runs before ids are computed, leaving `redactEvent`\n   743\t  as the only source of same-id different-content pairs.\n   744\t- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n   745\t  shared (notes-ref history rewrite + force-push + collaborator re-fetch\n   746\t  coordination). This is now the *only* path for already-shared content,\n   747\t  since `cledger redact` refuses it rather than pretending. Three hard parts\n   748\t  scoped so far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator\n   749\t  still holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so\n   750\t  purge must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction\n   751\t  and detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n   752\t  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n   753\t  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n   754\t  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n   755\t  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n   756\t  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n   757\t  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n   758\t- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n   759\t  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n   760\t  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n   761\t  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n   762\t  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n   763\t  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n   764\t  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n   765\t  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n   766\t  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n   767\t  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n   768\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n   769\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n   770\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n   771\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n   772\t  scope and capture triggers.\n   773\t- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n   774\t  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n   775\t  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n   776\t  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n   777\t  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n   778\t  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n   779\t  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n   780\t  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n   781\t  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n   782\t  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n   783\t  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n   784\t  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n   785\t- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n   786\t  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n   787\t  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n   788\t  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n   789\t  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n   790\t  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n   791\t  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n   792\t  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n   793\t  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n   794\t  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n   795\t  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n   796\t  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n   797\t  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n   798\t  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n   799\t  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n   800\t  intended narrow form.\n   801\t- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n   802\t  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n   803\t  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n   804\t  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n   805\t  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk — and it means `cledger redact --pattern`\n   806\t  currently *increases* the number of plaintext copies of a secret on local\n   807\t  disk, which is a surprising thing for a redaction command to do. Owner-only\n   808\t  `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1.\n   809\t  **Preferred fix: store hashes, not plaintext** (see the entry below) — it\n   810\t  removes the secret rather than protecting it, and needs no key at all.\n   811\t  The previously-planned alternative was encryption with an OS-keychain-held\n   812\t  key (macOS Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt\n   813\t  *non-interactively* since\n   814\t  capture runs silently on every turn.\n   815\t- **Store hashes of known secrets, not the secrets** — replace\n   816\t  `known-secrets.json`'s plaintext `values` with salted digests plus each\n   817\t  value's byte length. Capture-time scrubbing then confirms a candidate span\n   818\t  by hashing it, so the store stops being a readable aggregation of every\n   819\t  secret you have ever confirmed. Strictly better than encrypting it: an\n   820\t  encrypted store is reversible by design (capture must decrypt silently, so\n   821\t  the key has to be reachable by anything running as you), whereas a hashed\n   822\t  store does not contain the secret at all — not for an attacker, and not for\n   823\t  cledger. It also deletes the whole key-management problem, which is the\n   824\t  reason the encryption entry above has never been done.\n   825\t  *The design question is candidate generation* — you cannot search for a\n   826\t  value you cannot reconstruct, so something must propose the spans to hash.\n   827\t  Two options, and they compose:\n   828\t  (a) **Rule-generated candidates.** Run the broad scan-tier rules purely as\n   829\t  candidate *generators* and confirm each match against the stored digests.\n   830\t  Cheap — O(matches × stored) — and, importantly, the noise that disqualified\n   831\t  those rules from capture-time *rewriting* is harmless here, because the\n   832\t  hash comparison is exact: a false-positive candidate simply fails to match\n   833\t  and nothing is rewritten. Its limit is recall, and the limit bites exactly\n   834\t  where this feature earns its keep: a value the store holds *because no rule\n   835\t  recognized it* still generates no candidate, so it is never checked.\n   836\t  (b) **Length-indexed rolling scan.** Index the digests by length and sweep\n   837\t  each string with a rolling hash (Rabin–Karp), running sha256 only on a\n   838\t  window whose rolling hash hits. Linear in text size per distinct stored\n   839\t  length, no dependency on any rule matching, and it subsumes (a) entirely.\n   840\t  More code; the honest cost.\n   841\t  *Accepted limitation either way:* a digest is brute-forceable for\n   842\t  low-entropy values, so human-chosen passwords remain dictionary-attackable\n   843\t  while random API tokens do not. That trade is deliberate — this tool\n   844\t  operates on code repositories, where the realistic secret is a generated\n   845\t  token, and a leaked credential's real remedy is rotation regardless. Salt\n   846\t  per-store so the file is not rainbow-table-able, and note that storing\n   847\t  lengths leaks a little and makes the file a guess-checking oracle, the same\n   848\t  property any password-hash file has.\n   849\t  *Migration:* hash existing plaintext entries in place on first write.\n   850\t- **Share local state across worktrees** *(shipped, 0.13.0)* — cledger kept\n   851\t  its local state under `git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`, which in a linked\n   852\t  worktree is that worktree's *private* directory\n   853\t  (`<main>/.git/worktrees/<name>/`), not the repo's shared one. Git is doing\n   854\t  exactly what it documents; the wrong assumption was that a repo has one git\n   855\t  dir. Everything cledger stores there is repo-wide, so a worktree behaved\n   856\t  like a separate repo, in four ways of descending severity: (a) the pre-push\n   857\t  hook was installed where **git never runs it** — git resolves hooks against\n   858\t  the common dir only — so a repo whose first capture happened in a worktree\n   859\t  got a hook that looked installed and silently never fired; (b) the\n   860\t  known-secrets store was invisible, failing **open**: values the user had\n   861\t  taught capture-time redaction to scrub were written to the ledger in the\n   862\t  clear, with nothing reporting it; (c) the allowlist was invisible, failing\n   863\t  closed, so already-dismissed scan findings blocked the ledger push again\n   864\t  (measured on this repo: 213 findings from a worktree vs 0 from the main\n   865\t  checkout); (d) capture cursors reset, re-scanning transcripts from the top.\n   866\t  Fixed by resolving a `commonDir` (`--path-format=absolute --git-common-dir`,\n   867\t  with a manual-resolve fallback for git < 2.31) and hanging all local state\n   868\t  and the hook path off it. Placement is irrelevant to the fix — git treats a\n   869\t  worktree nested under the repo and one at an arbitrary path identically, and\n   870\t  both are covered by tests. *Fallout that outlived the cause:* (d) also\n   871\t  **duplicated events**, because append-time dedup only consults the note of\n   872\t  the commit being written to, so a re-scan after HEAD moved filed a second\n   873\t  copy under a different anchor; once both anchors were reachable, reads\n   874\t  returned the turn twice (measured: 351 duplicated ids in this repo's own\n   875\t  ledger). The ledger is append-only, so those copies are permanent — reads\n   876\t  now collapse events by id, preferring the earliest `recorded_at` with the\n   877\t  canonical serialization as a deterministic tie-break. That path is not\n   878\t  worktree-specific; any forced rescan after HEAD moves can produce it.\n   879\t  *Deliberately unchanged:* `.cledger.json` stays a working-tree file, so it\n   880\t  is per-worktree unless committed — correct for versioned repo config, unlike\n   881\t  `.git`-side state. *Not migrated:* an allowlist or known-secrets store\n   882\t  written from a worktree before this fix stays orphaned in that worktree's\n   883\t  private dir; re-run `cledger allow` / `cledger redact --pattern` if you hit\n   884\t  it.\n   885\t- **Record which model/provider/CLI version served each turn** *(shipped,\n   886\t  0.12.0)* — `producer` previously said only which capture path recorded an\n   887\t  event (`source`, `session_id`, cledger's own `tool`/`version`), never what\n   888\t  produced it: the model, the inference provider, or the coding CLI's\n   889\t  version. Codex's `session_meta`/`turn_context` lines carry exactly that and\n   890\t  were dropped with no trace; Claude Code's per-line `version` and\n   891\t  `message.model` survived only inside `raw.data`, unqueryable without\n   892\t  parsing a source-native shape. Three optional fields close it —\n   893\t  `producer.model`, `producer.provider`, `producer.source_version` — with\n   894\t  `cledger log --model M` to filter and the model(s) listed per row in\n   895\t  `cledger conversations`. *Only what the source states is recorded:*\n   896\t  `provider` stays unset for claude-code (never named in the transcript, and\n   897\t  the same CLI can point at the first-party API, Bedrock, or Vertex), a model\n   898\t  is never carried onto a turn the source did not label (so claude-code user\n   899\t  turns have none), and `\"<synthetic>\"` passes through verbatim rather than\n   900\t  being normalized away. Codex needed real work beyond reading a field: its\n   901\t  facts live on out-of-band lines, so a cursor-resumed capture re-scans the\n   902\t  file prefix to rebuild the context in force, and because `turn_context` is\n   903\t  written *after* the turn's opening messages in real rollouts, still-unset\n   904\t  fields are seeded from the earliest line stating them ahead — otherwise the\n   905\t  first user message of every session goes unlabelled. Seeding fills gaps\n   906\t  only; a genuine mid-session model switch still applies forward, never\n   907\t  retroactively. The fields are **excluded from event identity** (adding them\n   908\t  to the id would make a rescan duplicate every pre-upgrade turn instead of\n   909\t  deduping it), and scanning is unaffected since `scan`/capture-tier\n   910\t  redaction walk only `content` and `raw.data`. *Forward-only, and\n   911\t  deliberately no backfill:* rewriting `producer` on stored events would keep\n   912\t  their ids but change their note lines, and under `cat_sort_uniq` the\n   913\t  rewritten and original lines both survive a merge with any peer still\n   914\t  holding the old one — two copies of one event. Old claude-code events lose\n   915\t  nothing permanent (their whole source line is in `raw.data`); old codex\n   916\t  events do, and re-capturing them does not help, since they dedup by id.\n   917\t  *Out of scope:* the rest of what those codex lines carry — `base_instructions`\n   918\t  (the full system prompt), sandbox/approval policy, reasoning effort — is\n   919\t  still not captured. That is a separate question (whole-session\n   920\t  configuration, not per-turn agent identity) with its own size and\n   921\t  redaction implications; this cut answers only \"what served this turn\".\n   922\t- **Preserve provider-encrypted reasoning as opaque state** *(shipped,\n   923\t  0.10.0)* — Codex's `reasoning` response_items carry `encrypted_content`\n   924\t  that only OpenAI's servers can decrypt; the client just round-trips the\n   925\t  blob, and replaying it restores the model's hidden reasoning\n   926\t  provider-side. Capture previously dropped these outright, which conflicted\n   927\t  with the record-everything-just-in-case posture: we discarded them because\n   928\t  *we* can't read them, yet the one party who can (the provider) already saw\n   929\t  the plaintext. Preserving them keeps replay possible without making replay\n   930\t  behavior part of cledger's contract. Shipped shape: a new\n   931\t  `reasoning` kind, provider-agnostic (not Codex-specific), raw-only —\n   932\t  `content` carries only an opacity marker, the blob lives solely in `raw`.\n   933\t  Rides the ledger's normal default-on sync like every other kind (no special\n   934\t  carve-out). `cledger export` includes it by default (lossless dump);\n   935\t  `cledger log`/`show`/`conversations` hide it by default (`--with-reasoning`\n   936\t  to reveal) so ciphertext doesn't clutter human-facing transcripts. A\n   937\t  reasoning item's `summary` field (real, provider-decrypted plaintext when\n   938\t  reasoning-summary mode is on — distinct from `encrypted_content`) is split\n   939\t  out into an ordinary visible `conversation_turn` at the same seq, so it is\n   940\t  never hidden as if it were opaque. Capture-tier redaction and `cledger scan`\n   941\t  both exempt only the `encrypted_content` field from pattern-matching (it's\n   942\t  high-entropy by construction and this feature's entire point is preserving\n   943\t  it byte-exact — a coincidental rule match would silently corrupt it); every\n   944\t  other field, including `summary`, is scanned exactly like any other stored\n   945\t  content. Forward-only: sessions captured before 0.10.0 already had their\n   946\t  reasoning dropped and it's not recovered from what's on disk. Replay\n   947\t  behavior, validation, and open questions are owned by\n   948\t  [Turnbridge's technical design](https://github.com/evintunador/turnbridge/blob/main/docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md#encrypted-reasoning-replay).\n   949\t  *Deliberately out of scope here:* inter-agent\n   950\t  `agent_message` payloads still drop their embedded `encrypted_content`\n   951\t  blocks outright (same as before) rather than preserving them via this new\n   952\t  `reasoning` kind — same provider-withheld material, but embedded mid-line\n   953\t  rather than a standalone response_item, so preserving it needs its own\n   954\t  shape; tracked as a follow-up, not bundled into this cut. Also out of scope\n   955\t  at the time: no ledger event recorded which model/provider/CLI version\n   956\t  produced a given turn — a pre-existing gap in `Producer`, not specific to\n   957\t  reasoning, since closed in 0.12.0 (see the entry above). That gap mattered\n   958\t  most here: an encrypted blob is only replayable against the model that\n   959\t  produced it, so `reasoning` events captured before 0.12.0 carry ciphertext\n   960\t  with no record of where to send it.\n   961\t- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n   962\t- **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n   963\t  discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n   964\t  commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n   965\t  with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n   966\t  the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n   967\t  remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n   968\t  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n   969\t  lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n   970\t  extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n   971\t  piece.\n   972\t- **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n   973\t  that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n   974\t  event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n   975\t  conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n   976\t  \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n   977\t  reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n   978\t  forge credentials and per-host setup.\n   979\t- **Capturing file *content*, not just its digest** — `file_snapshot` records\n   980\t  the pointer a source states and the sha256 the ledger read from it at\n   981\t  capture time (see \"What gets recorded\"). That makes the record verifiable\n   982\t  and survives the cache being pruned, but a consumer who no longer has the\n   983\t  bytes still cannot reconstruct the file: the digest proves a copy is the\n   984\t  right one, it does not produce one. Inlining the bodies is the thorough fix\n   985\t  and the expensive one — unbounded ledger growth, whole source files through\n   986\t  the redaction stack — so it wants a size policy and probably an opt-in\n   987\t  before it ships. Raised by context-graph, which wants sub-commit file\n   988\t  history and reads `~/.claude/file-history/` directly today.\n   989\t- **Codex sub-agent coverage: confirmed, and it was corrupting the parent**\n   990\t  *(partly shipped, 0.24.0)* — the question was whether codex writes a separate\n   991\t  rollout for a sub-agent. **It does.** Measured on 40 local rollouts from\n   992\t  Codex 0.146.0: 11 are sub-agent threads, sitting in the same flat\n   993\t  `YYYY/MM/DD/` tree with no distinguishing filename. A child is told apart by\n   994\t  the shape of `session_meta.payload.source` — a plain string for a top-level\n   995\t  session (`cli` 22, `exec` 5, `vscode` 2), an object carrying a `subagent` key\n   996\t  for a child — and all 11 carry `parent_thread_id`. They hold real\n   997\t  conversation, not just activity: messages, reasoning, tool calls, and 185\n   998\t  `sub_agent_activity` records of their own, so the nesting is recursive.\n   999\t\n  1000\t  *Looking turned up a live bug, which is what actually shipped.* The adapter\n  1001\t  keyed a session on `session_meta.payload.session_id`, and **on a child\n  1002\t  rollout that field holds the parent's id**. Two silent consequences: a\n  1003\t  child's turns were filed into the parent's conversation while numbered by the\n  1004\t  child's own line index, interleaving nonsense into the parent's `seq`\n  1005\t  ordering; and the capture cursor, keyed on that same id, was shared between\n  1006\t  parent and child, each dragging the other into a full rescan. Keying on\n  1007\t  `payload.id` fixes both, and costs nothing: `payload.id` equals the\n  1008\t  filename's uuid for all 40 files while `session_id` does so for only the 29\n  1009\t  top-level ones, so every top-level event id is unchanged and only the\n  1010\t  children — mis-filed to begin with — move. A child now records\n  1011\t  `conversation.parent`, the same shape claude-code sidechains and opencode\n  1012\t  child sessions already use.\n  1013\t\n  1014\t  *Still open: discovery.* `event_msg/sub_agent_activity` carries an\n  1015\t  `agent_thread_id`, and 5 of the 6 distinct values seen resolve to a rollout\n  1016\t  file on disk (the sixth was presumably pruned), so\n  1017\t  `agent_thread_id` → `rollout-*-<id>.jsonl` is a working trail — the codex\n  1018\t  analogue of claude-code's `subagents/` sibling directory. cledger does not\n  1019\t  walk it: capture only ever reads the transcript it was handed. So a codex\n  1020\t  sub-agent is captured correctly **if** something hands cledger its rollout,\n  1021\t  and whether codex fires a hook per child thread is unverified — no codex\n  1022\t  session has been captured in this repo to check against. Until the walk\n  1023\t  exists, treat codex sub-agent capture as correct-but-not-guaranteed rather\n  1024\t  than as parity with the other two adapters.\n  1025\t- **The tail sweep is a workaround, not a fix** — a session cannot capture its\n  1026\t  own final lines, because the last hook runs before they are written. The\n  1027\t  claude-code hook therefore sweeps other transcripts in the project whose\n  1028\t  file grew past their cursor, which closes the gap on the *next* session in\n  1029\t  that repo. Two limits follow from that. A repo whose last session is never\n  1030\t  followed by another still ends with an uncaptured tail. And the sweep only\n  1031\t  considers transcripts cledger already has a cursor for, because a missing\n  1032\t  cursor means \"never captured\", not \"has a gap\" — so sessions predating the\n  1033\t  install are deliberately never adopted, and there is no backfill command\n  1034\t  that would adopt them on purpose either. A `SessionEnd`-with-delay would\n  1035\t  close the first properly; an explicit `cledger capture claude-code --all`,\n  1036\t  which anchors knowingly rather than silently, would close the second.\n  1037\t\n  1038\t## Storage model, in one paragraph\n","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","nl -ba README.md | sed -n '210,430p' && nl -ba README.md | sed -n '430,700p' && nl -ba README.md | sed -n '700,1038p'"],"cwd":"file:///Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","duration":{"nanos":8166,"secs":0},"exit_code":0,"formatted_output":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 19676)\nTotal output lines: 831\n\n   210\t## Adapters\n   211\t\n   212\tSupported today (built-in, per-turn, triggered by the CLI's own hook or\n   213\tplugin mechanism):\n   214\t\n   215\tEvery captured event records the agent that served it — `producer.model`,\n   216\t`producer.provider`, `producer.source_version` — but only where the source\n   217\tstates it; see the Roadmap entry below for what each adapter does and does\n   218\tnot know.\n   219\t\n   220\t| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n   221\t|---|---|---|---|\n   222\t| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. Every line type is now recorded: `system` lines that printed text become turns spoken by the system, `attachment` becomes `context_injection`, `mode`/`permission-mode`/`worktree-state`/`pr-link` and friends become `session_state`, `queue-operation` and the rest become `activity`, and `file-history-*` becomes `file_snapshot` with backup digests resolved at capture time. Sidechain (subagent) turns are captured as sub-conversations instead of dropped — including the `<session>/subagents/agent-*.jsonl` sibling files newer Claude Code writes, which the hook payload never names. The hook also