- **Record which model/provider/CLI version served each turn** *(shipped,\n   886\t  0.12.0)* — `producer` previously said only which capture path recorded an\n   887\t  event (`source`, `session_id`, cledger's own `tool`/`version`), never what\n   888\t  produced it: the model, the inference provider, or the coding CLI's\n   889\t  version. Codex's `session_meta`/`turn_context` lines carry exactly that and\n   890\t  were dropped with no trace; Claude Code's per-line `version` and\n   891\t  `message.model` survived only inside `raw.data`, unqueryable without\n   892\t  parsing a source-native shape. Three optional fields close it —\n   893\t  `producer.model`, `producer.provider`, `producer.source_version` — with\n   894\t  `cledger log --model M` to filter and the model(s) listed per row in\n   895\t  `cledger conversations`. *Only what the source states is recorded:*\n   896\t  `provider` stays unset for claude-code (never named in the transcript, and\n   897\t  the same CLI can point at the first-party API, Bedrock, or Vertex), a model\n   898\t  is never carried onto a turn the source did not label (so claude-code user\n   899\t  turns have none), and `\"<synthetic>\"` passes through verbatim rather than\n   900\t  being normalized away. Codex needed real work beyond reading a field: its\n   901\t  facts live on out-of-band lines, so a cursor-resumed capture re-scans the\n   902\t  file prefix to rebuild the context in force, and because `turn_context` is\n   903\t  written *after* the turn's opening messages in real rollouts, still-unset\n   904\t  fields are seeded from the earliest line stating them ahead — otherwise the\n   905\t  first user message of every session goes unlabelled. Seeding fills gaps\n   906\t  only; a genuine mid-session model switch still applies forward, never\n   907\t  retroactively. The fields are **excluded from event identity** (adding them\n   908\t  to the id would make a rescan duplicate every pre-upgrade turn instead of\n   909\t  deduping it), and scanning is unaffected since `scan`/capture-tier\n   910\t  redaction walk only `content` and `raw.data`. *Forward-only, and\n   911\t  deliberately no backfill:* rewriting `producer` on stored events would keep\n   912\t  their ids but change their note lines, and under `cat_sort_uniq` the\n   913\t  rewritten and original lines both survive a merge with any peer still\n   914\t  holding the old one — two copies of one event. Old claude-code events lose\n   915\t  nothing permanent (their whole source line is in `raw.data`); old codex\n   916\t  events do, and re-capturing them does not help, since they dedup by id.\n   917\t  *Out of scope:* the rest of what those codex lines carry — `base_instructions`\n   918\t  (the full system prompt), sandbox/approval policy, reasoning effort — is\n   919\t  still not captured. That is a separate question (whole-session\n   920\t  configuration, not per-turn agent identity) with its own size and\n   921\t  redaction implications; this cut answers only \"what served this turn\".\n   922\t- **Preserve provider-encrypted reasoning as opaque state** *(shipped,\n   923\t  0.10.0)* — Codex's `reasoning` response_items carry `encrypted_content`\n   924\t  that only OpenAI's servers can decrypt; the client just round-trips the\n   925\t  blob, and replaying it restores the model's hidden reasoning\n   926\t  provider-side. Capture previously dropped these outright, which conflicted\n   927\t  with the record-everything-just-in-case posture: we discarded them because\n   928\t  *we* can't read them, yet the one party who can (the provider) already saw\n   929\t  the plaintext. Preserving them keeps replay possible without making replay\n   930\t  behavior part of cledger's contract. Shipped shape: a new\n   931\t  `reasoning` kind, provider-agnostic (not Codex-specific), raw-only —\n   932\t  `content` carries only an opacity marker, the blob lives solely in `raw`.\n   933\t  Rides the ledger's normal default-on