r who no longer has the\n   983\t  bytes still cannot reconstruct the file: the digest proves a copy is the\n   984\t  right one, it does not produce one. Inlining the bodies is the thorough fix\n   985\t  and the expensive one — unbounded ledger growth, whole source files through\n   986\t  the redaction stack — so it wants a size policy and probably an opt-in\n   987\t  before it ships. Raised by context-graph, which wants sub-commit file\n   988\t  history and reads `~/.claude/file-history/` directly today.\n   989\t- **Codex sub-agent coverage: confirmed, and it was corrupting the parent**\n   990\t  *(partly shipped, 0.24.0)* — the question was whether codex writes a separate\n   991\t  rollout for a sub-agent. **It does.** Measured on 40 local rollouts from\n   992\t  Codex 0.146.0: 11 are sub-agent threads, sitting in the same flat\n   993\t  `YYYY/MM/DD/` tree with no distinguishing filename. A child is told apart by\n   994\t  the shape of `session_meta.payload.source` — a plain string for a top-level\n   995\t  session (`cli` 22, `exec` 5, `vscode` 2), an object carrying a `subagent` key\n   996\t  for a child — and all 11 carry `parent_thread_id`. They hold real\n   997\t  conversation, not just activity: messages, reasoning, tool calls, and 185\n   998\t  `sub_agent_activity` records of their own, so the nesting is recursive.\n   999\t\n  1000\t  *Looking turned up a live bug, which is what actually shipped.* The adapter\n  1001\t  keyed a session on `session_meta.payload.session_id`, and **on a child\n  1002\t  rollout that field holds the parent's id**. Two silent consequences: a\n  1003\t  child's turns were filed into the parent's conversation while numbered by the\n  1004\t  child's own line index, interleaving nonsense into the parent's `seq`\n  1005\t  ordering; and the capture cursor, keyed on that same id, was shared between\n  1006\t  parent and child, each dragging the other into a full rescan. Keying on\n  1007\t  `payload.id` fixes both, and