t  bookkeeping does not renumber the turns around it. `--json` and `export` are\n   498\t  untouched and still carry the stored `seq`: a machine reading the ledger\n   499\t  wants the real ordering key, not a per-view position. Ties share a rank,\n   500\t  since a reasoning blob and the turn it belongs to are deliberately written at\n   501\t  the same `seq`.\n   502\t\n   503\t  *Migration:* forward-only, like every identity change here. Already-captured\n   504\t  opencode events keep their positional seqs; only newly captured parts use the\n   505\t  derived ones, and the two sort correctly together (small numbers first, which\n   506\t  is also their real order). Re-capturing an old session from scratch would\n   507\t  file second copies — the same one-round cost every identity change in this\n   508\t  project carries, and bounded here by how few opencode events exist relative\n   509\t  to a whole ledger.\n   510\t\n   511\t  Related: opencode's `session delete` removes a conversation from opencode\n   512\t  entirely while the ledger keeps it, which is a feature, not a bug.\n   513\t- **The opencode hook's session-id fallback is untested** — the plugin reads\n   514\t  `event.properties.sessionID`, confirmed against a live `session.idle` from\n   515\t  opencode 1.18.5, so the normal path is pinned to the real field. The hook\n   516\t  still treats the id as optional and falls back to capturing the most\n   517\t  recently updated session for the directory, so a future opencode that\n   518\t  renames the field degrades instead of silently stopping. That fallback has\n   519\t  never run in anger, and it would pick the wrong session if two sessions in\n   520\t  one project went idle in the same instant. Worth a test that exercises it\n   521\t  directly, and a warning when it triggers, so a silent rename does not look\n   522\t  like normal operation.\n   523\t- **`opencode export` truncates a piped stdout at 64KB** — an upstream Bun\n   524\t  flush bug: the process exits without draining stdout, so any session over\n   525\t  ~64KB comes back cut off, and the truncated JSON can still parse. cledger\n   526\t  works around it by routing the child's stdout to a temp file rather than a\n   527\t  pipe (see `runOpencode`). Worth reporting upstream; the workaround should\n   528\t  stay regardless, since it costs nothing and protects older opencode\n   529\t  versions.\n   530\t- **Config sections replace rather than merge, and it fails open** — *fixed in\n   531\t  0.21.0*: `loadConfig` now merges each top-level section key-by-key, so a\n   532\t  repo `.cledger.json` that sets only `redact.patterns` no longer silently\n   533\t  reverts a globally-enabled `redact.knownSecrets` to its default. The one\n   534\t  deliberate exception: `scan.allowFingerprints` arrays are unioned rather\n   535\t  than repo-wins, because the allowlist is accumulative — a fingerprint either\n   536\t  config trusts is trusted, and replacement would make a repo-local entry\n   537\t  silently drop every personal one. Kept here rather than deleted because the\n   538\t  bug is a family member (see below) and its shape — a config surface where\n   539\t  *mentioning* a section disabled a sibling protection — is worth remembering\n   540\t  when adding config keys.\n   541\t\n   542\t  **Self-referential failures are now this project's characteristic bug\n   543\t  family**, and are worth a standing check rather than four independent\n   544\t  fixes. cledger records the work of building cledger, so its own state,\n   545\t  output, and documentation feed back into its behavior. Known members: the\n   546\t  capture/scan redaction loop (a finding report re-seeding the finding it\n   547\t  describes — closed in 0.6.1/0.7.0); the pre-0.13.0 worktree state split\n   548\t  (0.13.0); this config section-replace; and the allowlist entry below, whose\n   549\t  *write-up* of a false positive reproduces the false positive in every repo\n   550\t  whose conversations quote it. Three of the four fail silently, and two fail\n   551\t  open. A useful tripwire when adding anything that reads cledger's own\n   552\t  state or prints cledger's own findings: ask what happens when that output\n   553\t  is itself captured.\n   554\t- **Don't print findings by default; print a pointer to them** — the cleanest\n   555\t  structural break in the self-referential loop above. Today `cledger scan`\n   556\t  and the sync gate print every finding inline, which is exactly the text that\n   557\t  gets captured into the next conversation and re-seeds the finding. Masking\n   558\t  the matched span (0.6.1) removed the secret characters but not the\n   559\t  *surrounding* credential-shaped context, which is what\n   560\t  `keyword-assignment` trips on — so the loop is narrowed, not closed.\n   561\t  Proposed: report only the count and how to inspect, with the audience split\n   562\t  made explicit in the text — a human is told which command reveals the\n   563\t  findings; an agent is told not to run it, because reading the findings into\n   564\t  its context is what reproduces them, and to surface the count to its human\n   565\t  instead. Naming the failure mode in the output is the point: an agent that\n   566\t  understands *why* will comply, where a bare \"don't look\" invites a\n   567\t  workaround. Pairs naturally with the inspection command in the design doc's\n   568\t  open questions, which already has to write unmasked output to a file rather\n   569\t  than stdout for the same reason. *Tension to resolve first:* CI reads those\n   570\t  printed findings, and `cledger scan`'s nonzero exit plus inline report is\n   571\t  the documented CI integration — so the default likely has to be\n   572\t  terminal-aware, or CI has to opt in explicitly (`--report`), rather than\n   573\t  simply going quiet for everyone.\n   574\t- **Branch-scoped push** *(shipped, 0.14.0)* — reachability filtered *reads*\n   575\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, `conversations`), but the notes ref is a single ref\n","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","nl -ba README.md | sed -n '307,575p'"],"cwd":"file:///Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","duration":{"nanos":12458,"secs":0},"exit_code":0,"formatted_output":"   307\t### Maximum safety recipe\n   308\t\n   309\tKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n   310\t\n   311\t## Roadmap\n   312\t\n   313\t- **Auto-triage of findings whose span is committed repo content — considered\n   314\t  and deferred, with data** (2026-08-12). The idea: if a flagged span exists\n   315\t  verbatim in a blob already on the remote, the ledger leaks nothing the repo\n   316\t  has not published, so the finding could downgrade itself. Tested against\n   317\t  the only backlog available — this repo's 11 outstanding fingerprints plus\n   318\t  turnbridge's 4 — it would have cleared **zero of 15**: the split-parts\n   319\t  corpus convention means assembled fixture bytes never reach a committed\n   320\t  file, and the other spans lived in shell commands and intermediate edit\n   321\t  states that were rewritten before committing. The mechanism is sound but\n   322\t  its hit rate on real backlogs is unproven, and it adds a scan-time\n   323\t  dependency on remote-reachability computation. Revisit if a backlog ever\n   324\t  shows committed-content spans; `cledger review` clearing 15 spans in a\n   325\t  minute weakens the case for more machinery.\n   326\t- **`cledger review` shows a context window, not the conversation** — the\n   327\t  0.21.0 reviewer renders the flagged string with ±600 chars (scrollable,\n   328\t  the whole string is available). A richer version would page through the\n   329\t  *conversation* around the event — prior and following turns, like reading\n   330\t  the transcript — which needs event-sequence pagination keyed on\n   331\t  `conversation.seq` and a real layout pass. Deferred until the per-span\n   332\t  flow proves insufficient; most verdicts so far need only the surrounding\n   333\t  string.\n   334\t- **The `>/dev/null 2>&1` on the Gemini and Qwen hooks was pointless, and cost\n   335\t  visibility** *(shipped, 0.22.0)* — it was added after `qwen -p \"...\"` failed\n   336\t  to capture, on the theory that the redirect \"routes the command through a\n   337\t  shell so the work outlives the teardown\". That theory is wrong: both CLIs\n   338\t  *always* invoke a hook as `bash -c \"<command>\"`, and `bash -c` `exec`s a\n   339\t  single simple command rather than forking, so nothing was ever detached. The\n   340\t  actual cause of hooks dying was the timeout unit below. All the redirect ever\n   341\t  did was hide the capture's own output, so a format-drift warning had nowhere\n   342\t  to print and `cledger capture <source> --all` was the only way to watch one.\n   343\t  New installs no longer write it, and `cledger install` **strips it from an\n   344\t  existing hook in place** — the same contract the timeout repair established:\n   345\t  a setting cledger itself got wrong should be fixable by re-running install,\n   346\t  not by hand-editing JSON. Only cledger's own hook is touched, and only when\n   347\t  the command ends in that exact string, so any other redirect a user wrote\n   348\t  survives. *Still unverified:* the original headless `qwen -p` case has not\n   349\t  been re-run, because that needs a live Qwen session; the reasoning above\n   350\t  says it was never the redirect that saved it, but nobody has watched it\n   351\t  since.\n   352\t- **`cledger log` hid human-driven records by default** *(shipped, 0.22.0)* —\n   353\t  the session-machinery kinds (`session_state`, `activity`,\n   354\t  `context_injection`, `file_snapshot`) were hidden unless you passed\n   355\t  `--with-state`. That default earns its keep against telemetry: one real Qwen\n   356\t  session emitted 57 `ui_telemetry` records against 45 events of actual\n   357\t  content, and an unfiltered default would bury the conversation. But it did\n   358\t  not distinguish *whose* action a record was, so a slash command the human\n   359\t  typed was hidden by the same rule as a token-count ping.\n   360\t  The display default is now keyed on the **actor**: a machinery event whose\n   361\t  actor is `human` shows, the harness's and the agent's bookkeeping stays\n   362\t  behind `--with-state`, and the flag's meaning is unchanged (show everything).\n   363\t  Capture is untouched and `cledger export` is still lossless — this only ever\n   364\t  changed what is displayed.\n   365\t  *The premise needed repair before the fix meant anything.* \"`activity`\n   366\t  already carries a real actor\" was half true: `activityDraft` was the only one\n   367\t  of the four builders that even took an actor, and exactly one call site in\n   368\t  the whole codebase passed `human` (Qwen's `slash_command`). Shipping only the\n   369\t  display change would have surfaced that one record and nothing else. Two\n   370\t  attributions were corrected alongside it, both where the source *states* who\n   371\t  acted: Gemini's `$rewindTo` is the person saying \"take that back\" (its\n   372\t  sibling `snapshot_drop` — Gemini quietly rewriting its own document — stays\n   373\t  `system`, and that difference is the entire reason the ledger records them as\n   374\t  two things), and Qwen's `at_command` is a person naming a file, which was\n   375\t  landing in `context_injection` with the identical `system` actor as\n   376\t  `agent_bootstrap`, the harness's own preamble that nobody typed.\n   377\t  `contextInjectionDraft` grew an actor parameter for that, matching\n   378\t  `activityDraft`.\n   379\t  *Deliberately not corrected: claude-code's `queue-operation`.* It looked like\n   380\t  the same fix — a queued message is something a human typed mid-turn — but the\n   381\t  source does not say so. Measured across 40 local transcripts: `enqueue`\n   382\t  splits 126 harness-generated to 43 human-typed, and `remove` 70 to 16. The\n   383\t  only available discriminator is a content-shape heuristic (harness ones\n   384\t  arrive wrapped in a `task-notification` envelope), which is a convention that\n   385\t  can change, and getting it wrong files machine-authored text under a named\n   386\t  person's git identity — the failure this whole entry is about, in the\n   387\t  direction that actually does damage. Attribution follows what the source\n   388\t  states; where it must be guessed from content, it stays `system`. Revisit if\n   389\t  claude-code ever labels the queue entry's origin.\n   390\t  *Forward-only, like every attribution change here.* `actor.type` and\n   391\t  `actor.id` are part of event identity, so a re-capture of an\n   392\t  already-captured session mints new ids for the re-attributed records rather\n   393\t  than deduping. That is id churn in one adapter's machinery records, not lost\n   394\t  content, and there is no backfill — rewriting stored events would put two\n   395\t  copies of each through `cat_sort_uniq` on the next merge with any peer.\n   396\t- **Gemini CLI's version is not in its session file** — claude-code and\n   397\t  qwen-code stamp the CLI version onto every line, so `producer.source_version`\n   398\t  comes for free. Gemini stamps none, and neither the hook payload nor the\n   399\t  session header carries one, so gemini-cli events have no `source_version`\n   400\t  unless a caller passes it. Reading it off the installed binary would be\n   401\t  wrong for a backfill of an old session, and guessing is worse than omitting.\n   402\t  Fixing it properly means asking Gemini to record it, or accepting a\n   403\t  capture-time \"the binary on PATH said 0.53.1\" annotation that is explicitly\n   404\t  about the capture rather than the session.\n   405\t- **`timeout` means different units in Claude Code than in the CLIs that copied\n   406\t  its hook format** — Claude Code reads a command hook's `timeout` as seconds;\n   407\t  Gemini CLI and Qwen Code pass the same field straight to `setTimeout`, so\n   408\t  theirs is milliseconds (`Hook timed out after ${timeout}ms`,\n   409\t  `DEFAULT_HOOK_TIMEOUT = 6e4`). `cledger install` wrote Claude's `120` into all\n   410\t  three, so on Gemini and Qwen every hook was SIGTERMed after 120 *milliseconds*\n   411\t  — less than a cold capture takes. The symptom was a CLI reporting\n   412\t  `Hook(s) [...] failed` on nearly every turn while its events sometimes still\n   413\t  landed, because whether the append finished before the signal was a race.\n   414\t  Fixed by writing each CLI's own unit, and `install` now repairs an existing\n   415\t  hook's timeout in place rather than seeing \"already installed\" and doing\n   416\t  nothing. If another CLI adopts this config shape, check its unit before\n   417\t  assuming.\n   418\t- **Signing in to Gemini CLI now needs a key** — Google refuses\n   419\t  `oauth-personal` sign-in for individuals on this client (*\"no longer\n   420\t  supported for Gemini Code Assist for individuals… migrate to the Antigravity\n   421\t  suite\"*), and that refusal is server-side, not a client change. The shipped\n   422\t  CLI still supports `gemini-api-key`, `vertex-ai` and `cloud-shell`, and\n   423\t  honours `GOOGLE_GEMINI_BASE_URL`. Note it has no OpenAI-compatible mode,\n   424\t  unlike Qwen Code (`OPENAI_BASE_URL`), so a local endpoint has to speak the\n   425\t  Gemini API. The transcript format is stable across the change — Gemini CLI\n   426\t  0.54.0's record predicates are byte-identical to 0.53.1's.\n   427\t- **Most Qwen `system` subtypes are classified by name, not by observed shape**\n   428\t  — live sessions have now exercised `ui_telemetry`, `attribution_snapshot`,\n   429\t  `file_history_snapshot`, `at_command` and `slash_command`. The rest of the\n   430\t  mapping (`custom_title` and `goal_state` → `session_state`,\n   431\t  `agent_bootstrap`/`agent_launch_prompt` → `context_injection`, and the\n   432\t  subtypes that fall through to `activity`) is still read off Qwen's own\n   433\t  `KNOWN_RECORD_SUBTYPES` list rather than off a transcript that produced them.\n   434\t  Notably `/chat save <title>` did *not* emit `custom_title` — it emitted a\n   435\t  pair of `slash_command` records — so the `custom_title` path remains\n   436\t  unobserved and may belong to some other gesture. Fields pass through verbatim\n   437\t  either way, so the risk is a record filed under a slightly wrong kind, not\n   438\t  lost or mangled content, but a subtype that turns out to be state while\n   439\t  sitting in `activity` would be invisible to a consumer filtering on\n   440\t  `session_state`.\n   441\t- **`cledger export | head` died with an unhandled EPIPE** *(shipped, 0.22.0)*\n   442\t  — `printJsonl` wrote to `process.stdout` with no `error` handler, so when the\n   443\t  reader went away mid-write Node threw and printed a stack trace instead of\n   444\t  exiting quietly. Not exotic: piping any ledger larger than the 64KB pipe\n   445\t  buffer into `head`, or into a command that fails at startup, reproduced it —\n   446\t  `cledger export | head -1` against a 23MB ledger threw every time. Small\n   447\t  ledgers hid it, because the whole payload fits the buffer before the reader\n   448\t  exits and no write ever fails.\n   449\t  Fixed with an `EPIPE` handler that exits 0, the convention every\n   450\t  `head`-friendly CLI follows; any other stdout error still reports and exits\n   451\t  1. The handler is installed for the whole CLI rather than around the three\n   452\t  JSON printers, since `log`'s human format and `conversations` write to the\n   453\t  same stdout and 