de the value —\n    13\t  `FAKE`, `EXAMPLE`, `PLACEHOLDER`, `DUMMY`, `NOTREAL`, or `TESTONLY`.\n    14\t  `password=FAKEhunter2aa` never becomes a finding; `password=hunter2aa1234`\n    15\t  becomes one the moment you type it, in the file *and* in this transcript.\n    16\t- **Format-valid tokens** (a real-looking `ghp_…`/`sk-ant-…` that must\n    17\t  exercise a capture rule): never write one whole — not in a file, not in\n    18\t  the chat, not in a commit message. Store it split as `secret_parts` in\n    19\t  `src/test/fixtures/secret-corpus.json` and let the test reassemble it\n    20\t  (see `src/test/secret-corpus.test.ts`). Markers do not exempt capture-tier\n    21\t  formats, deliberately.\n    22\t- Existing helpers already follow this; extend them rather than minting\n    23\t  values inline (`fakeSecret(...)` in `src/test/redact.test.ts`, the corpus\n    24\t  file for anything new).\n    25\t\n    26\tIf a scan finding does appear when pushing: **stop and tell the human.** Do\n    27\tnot run `cledger review`, `cledger inspect`, or `cledger export` to look at\n    28\tit — reading flagged content into this conversation re-seeds the finding.\n    29\tThe human clears the queue with `cledger review` in a plain terminal.\n    30\t\n    31\t## The standing tripwire\n    32\t\n    33\tcledger records the work of building cledger. Before adding anything that\n    34\tprints findings, reads cledger's own state, or writes examples about either,\n    35\task: what happens when this output is itself captured? (See \"Self-referential\n    36\tfailures\" in the README roadmap.)\n   687\t## Open questions\n   688\t\n   689\t- Path-based capture exclusion (the path half of layer D): requires\n   690\t  correlating tool_use file paths with their tool_result events.\n   691\t- Post-push purge tooling (force-push the notes ref + collaborator\n   692\t  re-fetch coordination).\n   693\t- Transport currently wires `origin` only; fork workflows (per-remote\n   694\t  staging refs, refspecs on other remotes) are unhandled — the pre-push\n   695\t  hook does push to whichever remote is being pushed, but fetch staging is\n   696\t  origin-scoped.\n   697\t- Sub-turn citation anchors for downstream consumers (intent-recall).\n   698\t- Re-anchoring shipped as default behavior in 0.8.0, and its suggestion\n   699\t  tier in 0.9.0 (see \"Squash merges and history rewrites\"): unmatched\n   700\t  branches get evidence-ranked candidates — the forge's own merge-commit\n   701\t  record for the branch's PR (GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session in\n   702\t  `src/forge/`, degrading to offline evidence without it), `(#N)` subject\n   703\t  and squash-message corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap — printed\n   704\t  with the conversation-carrying commits named and a ready-to-run `--onto`\n   705\t  command; confirm-only, and forge lookups never run in the auto read path.\n   706\t  Remaining: the accepted cursor gap, where conversations captured onto an\n   707\t  already-dead branch only auto-map on the next target move (`cledger\n   708\t  re-anchor` covers it manually).\n   709\t- Whether to preserve non-git-controlled harness artifacts that die with a\n   710\t  worktree (agent memory directories, session state) as `document` events.\n   711\t- Per-turn agent provenance (`producer.model`/`provider`/`source_version`)\n   712\t  shipped in 0.12.0; see \"Agent provenance\". The follow-up — the rest of what\n   713\t  codex's `session_meta`/`turn_context` lines carry (sandbox/approval policy,\n   714\t  reasoning effort, workspace roots) — is answered by `session_state`: those\n   715\t  lines are now recorded whole, in addition to being read for `producer`.\n   716\t- Whether an explicit stream/conversation manifest object earns its keep once\n   717\t  multiple producers exist.\n   718\t- Reads deliberately do *not* walk the notes ref's history; only the tip tree\n   719\t  is authoritative. Unioning across history was proposed and set aside because\n   720\t  it defeats redaction: the notes ref's ancestry retains every superseded note\n   721\t  body, so a history-walking read would surface exactly the pre-redaction\n   722\t  content. It would also break `git notes show` interop (standard tooling\n   723\t  reads the tip tree) and make reads O(notes history). Two related weaknesses\n   724\t  that were open here have since been closed: `dedupById` no longer prefers\n   725\t  the un-redacted copy (redaction depth now outranks both `recorded_at` and\n   726\t  the byte tie-break), and `cledger redact` now refuses already-pushed events\n   727\t  outright instead of skipping the squash and proceeding. What remains by\n   728\t  design is the local reflog/object residue after a pre-push redaction, which\n   729\t  never leaves the machine.\n   730\t- Per-branch *storage* (a notes ref per branch) has been considered and set\n   731\t  aside, but the question it was reaching for is open. Branch membership is a\n   732\t  derived property of the commit DAG, not an intrinsic property of a commit:\n   733\t  one commit is on many branches at once, and merges, rebases, renames,\n   734\t  cherry-picks and deletions all change the answer without touching the\n   735\t  commit. Anchoring to commits and computing reachability at read time keeps\n   736\t  that derivation where git already maintains it; storing it per branch would\n   737\t  denormalize it and require synchronizing on every one of those operations.\n   738\t  Squash merge is the decisive case — it deletes the source branch outright,\n## cleanup/remove-migration-tooling...origin/cleanup/remove-migration-tooling\n","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","nl -ba package.json | sed -n '1,80p' && nl -ba src/ledger.ts && nl -ba src/store.ts | sed -n '1,140p' && nl -ba src/renormalize.ts | sed -n '1,110p' && nl -ba CLAUDE.md | sed -n '1,45p' && nl -ba docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md | sed -n '687,738p' && git status --short --branch"],"cwd":"file:///User