echnical design\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:5:## Scope\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:16:## Canonical objects\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:64:### Agent provenance\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:158:## Storage\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:183:### Push scope\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:295:### Squash merges and history rewrites: re-anchoring\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:336:## Transport\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:365:## Capture adapters\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:411:## Privacy and integrity\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:421:### Redaction layers\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:532:### Redaction metadata\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:581:## Versioning against harness format changes\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:687:## Open questions\ndocs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md:1:# Product intent\ndocs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md:3:## Purpose\ndocs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md:15:## Behavioral commitments\ndocs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md:27:## Non-goals for the foundation\nREADME.md:1:# Conversation Ledger\nREADME.md:39:## Install\nREADME.md:73:## Use\nREADME.md:89:## Sharing (transport)\nREADME.md:118:## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\nREADME.md:150:## What gets recorded\nREADME.md:179:### Sub-conversations\nREADME.md:196:### File snapshots and `resolved`\nREADME.md:210:## Adapters\nREADME.md:247:## Security & redaction\nREADME.md:251:### What cledger does and does not protect\nREADME.md:262:### Writing secret-shaped fixtures\nREADME.md:269:### Redaction layers\nREADME.md:295:### Commands\nREADME.md:307:### Maximum safety recipe\nREADME.md:311:## Roadmap\nREADME.md:1038:## Storage model, in one paragraph\nREADME.md:1050:## License\n\nCLAUDE:\n# Working in conversation-ledger\n\n## Writing secret-shaped fixtures (read before adding any test or doc example)\n\nThis project tests secret redaction, so its tests and docs constantly need\nfake credentials — and this very conversation is being captured by cledger,\nso every fake credential you type becomes ledger content that the pre-push\nscan will flag and a human will have to review. Author fixtures so they never\nenter that queue:\n\n- **Any keyword-shaped fake** (`password=...`, `api_key: ...`, URL\n  credentials, bearer tokens): put an uppercase marker inside the value —\n  `FAKE`, `EXAMPLE`, `PLACEHOLDER`, `DUMMY`, `NOTREAL`, or `TESTONLY`.\n  `password=FAKEhunter2aa` never becomes a finding; `password=hunter2aa1234`\n  becomes one the moment you type it, in the file *and* in this transcript.\n- **Format-valid tokens** (a real-looking `ghp_…`/`sk-ant-…` that must\n  exercise a capture rule): never write one whole — not in a file, not in\n  the chat, not in a commit message. Store it split as `secret_parts` in\n  `src/test/fixtures/secret-corpus.json` and let the test reassemble it\n  (see `src/test/secret-corpus.test.ts`). Markers do not exempt capture-tier\n  formats, deliberately.\n- Existing helpers already follow this; extend them rather than minting\n  values inline (`fakeSecret(...)` in `src/test/redact.test.ts`, the corpus\n  file for anything new).\n\nIf a scan finding does appear when pushing: **stop and tell the human.** Do\nnot run `cledger review`, `cledger inspect`, or `cledger export` to look at\nit — reading flagged content into this conversation re-seeds the finding.\nThe human clears the queue with `cledger review` in a plain terminal.\n\n## The standing tripwire\n\ncledger records the work of building cledger. Before adding anything that\nprints findings, reads cledger's own state, or writes examples about either,\nask: what happens when this output is itself captured? (See \"Self-referential\nfailures\" in the README roadmap.)\n\nCurrent branch diff from main:\n README.md                       |  15 --\n scripts/migrate-ev1-history.mjs | 327 ----------------------------------------\n src/test/migration.test.ts      | 120 ---------------\n 3 files changed, 462 deletions(-)\nM\tREADME.md\nD\tscripts/migrate-ev1-history.mjs\nD\tsrc/test/migration.test.ts\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},"timestamp":"2026-09-24T02:16:18.696Z","type":"response_item"},"format":"codex-rollout-jsonl/2"},"recorded_at":"2026-09-24T02:18:37.566Z","schema":"annals/v1","stream":{"id":"codex:01a0d132-3af5-7f63-9ce8-f92787b93442","seq":37}}
{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"Stale/local-path references:\nREADME.md:267:- **For format-valid tokens** (a syntactically real `ghp_…` that must exercise a capture rule), markers are ignored by design — store the token split into parts and reassemble at runtime, as `src/test/fixtures/secret-corpus.json` does with `secret_parts`. A whole format-valid token should never exist as literal bytes in a file, a conversation, or a commit.\nREADME.md:767:  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\nREADME.md:968:  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\nCLAUDE.md:19:  `src/test/fixtures/secret-corpus.json` and let the test reassemble it\nCLAUDE.md:20:  (see `src/test/secret-corpus.test.ts`). Markers do not exempt capture-tier\nCLAUDE.md:23:  values inline (`fakeSecret(...)` in `src/test/redact.test.ts`, the corpus\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:702:  `src/forge/`, degrading to offline evidence without it), `(#N)` subject\n\nCLI usage:\nconst USAGE = `conversation-ledger — durable records of coding-agent conversations, in git notes\n\nUsage:\n  cledger append [--quiet]                 append JSONL events/drafts from stdin\n  cledger log [--all|--rev R] [--kind K] [--source S] [--model M] [--conversation C] [--json]\n              [--with-reasoning] [--with-state]\n                                           --model matches producer.model exactly, e.g. gpt-5.6-sol\n  cledger show <conversation-id-prefix> [--json] [--with-reasoning] [--with-state]\n                                           opaque provider-encrypted \\`reasoning\\` events are hidden\n                                           by default on log/show; --with-reasoning reveals them.\n                                           So are the session-machinery kinds (session_state,\n                                           activity, context_injection, file_snapshot), which a\n                                           session emits far more often than turns — except the\n                                           ones the source attributes to a human (a slash command,\n                                           an @-command, an explicit rewind), which show by\n                                           default; --with-state reveals the harness's and the\n                                           agent's bookkeeping too.\n                                           Both are always captured and exported —\n                                           these flags only affect what is displayed\n  cledger conversations [--rev R] [--with-reasoning] [--with-state]\n                                           list conversations on current branch (--all for every branch),\n                                           one line each: id, source, model(s), count, time span\n  cledger export [--all|--rev R]          lossless JSONL dump of every field, incl. reasoning;\n                                           scoped to the current branch like log, --all for the\n                                           whole local ledger\n  cledger sync [--remote R] [--push|--fetch] [--no-scan] [--paranoid] [--all|--rev R]\n                                           fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref;\n                                           push is gated by a secret scan unless --no-scan.\n                                           Push carries only conversations reachable from the\n                                           current branch; --all pushes the whole ledger,\n                                           --rev scopes to another branch/commit\n  cledger transport-push [remote]         pre-push hook entrypoint (installed automatically):\n                                           pushes the ledger ref alongside git push, scoped to the\n                                           refs being pushed (read from git's pre-push stdin;\n                                           falls back to HEAD); scan findings hold back only the\n                                           ledger unless transport.strict\n  cledger scan [--all|--rev R] [--paranoid]   scan local events for potential secrets (CI-friendly:\n                                           exits 1 if any finding, 0 otherwise); default scope is\n                                           every local event, --rev restricts by reachability\n  cledger review [--paranoid] [--context N]   step through every outstanding finding interactively:\n                                           one screen per distinct span, match highlighted in\n                                           context, one key to allow (here or globally), redact\n                                           everywhere, or skip. HUMANS ONLY, plain terminal —\n                                           refuses inside an agent session and without a TTY.\n  cledger inspect <event-id-prefix> [--context N] [--reveal] [--stdout] [--force]\n                                           show a finding with real surrounding context (default 400\n                                           chars each side). Writes to a file outside the repo and\n                                           refuses to run inside a coding-agent session — reports\n                                           never print content, because reprinting it re-seeds the\n                                           finding. HUMANS ONLY, in a plain terminal.\n  cledger allow <fingerprint...> [--global]   mark scan finding fingerprint(s) as known false\n                                           positives, for this repo or (--global) every repo on\n                                           this machine; repos can also commit shared entries in\n                                           .cledger.json {\"scan\":{\"allowFingerprints\":[...]}}\n  cledger redact <event-id-prefix> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]\n                                           rewrite a